<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">12 May 1789 · House of Commons, London</p><h1 class="sp-title">Wilberforce against the Slave Trade</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce">William Wilberforce</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Persuaded by the campaigner Thomas Clarkson and encouraged by his friend William Pitt the Younger, the young member of Parliament William Wilberforce opened the case against the British slave trade in a speech of more than three hours, drawing on the evidence that abolitionists had gathered about the Middle Passage.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>So enormous, so dreadful, so irremediable did its wickedness appear that my own mind was completely made up for the abolition.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Speech to the House of Commons, 12 May 1789.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Speech_of_William_Wilberforce,_Esq.,_Representative_for_the_County_of_York,_on_Wednesday_the_13th_of_May,_1789,_on_the_Question_of_the_Abolition_of_the_Slave_Trade" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource (pamphlet of 1789)</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>The Commons put off a decision, and Wilberforce's bills failed again and again for eighteen years. Parliament abolished the British slave trade in 1807, and Wilberforce died in July 1833, three days after the Commons passed the bill to abolish slavery throughout most of the British Empire.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abolition-of-the-atlantic-slave-trade">Abolition of the slave trade (1807)</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery-abolition-act-1833">Slavery Abolition Act 1833</a></div></div></section>
